  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=5f(3x-6)+2=5f(3(x-2))+2\n$$\n\nTo get $y=5f(3(x-2))+2,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(3x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}5).$ Translate $y=f(3x)$ right by $2$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(3(x-2)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(3(x-2))$ by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=5f(3(x-2)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=5f(3(x-2))$ up by $2$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=5f(3(x-2))+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}5)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3).$",
